dcomp: composite each tick into an off-screen frame and copy it to the window once

The DirectComposition compositor (dcomp-steinberg) drew every visual of a
tick straight onto the target window's DC, one visual after another. The
window's surface can reach the screen between two of those blits, so a frame
that is composited but not finished shows up: the base visual without the
overlay visual drawn on top of it. In Cubase 14 that is the project cursor
line, which flickered on and off (two frames bright, two dim, about 15 Hz in
a 60 fps recording) with the project stopped.

Compose into an off-screen bitmap instead: copy the window's current content
in (so what no visual draws is kept), let do_composite() draw every visual
into it, and BitBlt the result to the window once. The bitmap is kept on the
target while the window size holds, and falls back to the old direct drawing
when it cannot be made.

Layers on dcomp-steinberg. Candidate for upstreaming together with it.

Tested with Cubase 14 on wine-staging 11.17: the cursor line no longer
flickers, and idle CPU after start dropped (about 6.3 cores against 8.6 in a
10 s sample taken just after start; start-up scanning is part of both).

